### Page 9 of 13

```text
Lot 14, Block 5, Replat Homestead Addition, First Filing Zone Change
UDC-18-00070

P a g e | 8

2. SURROUNDING AREA - The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering
existing zoning, the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies created under the
guidance of that plan for these areas.
The application is compatible with the zoning of
nearby property. The property is currently zoned
County MR Medium Density Residential and, if
annexed by the Governing Body, would need a
City zoning district to be developed. This
application proposes rezoning the land outlined in
red in Map #5 to NR-2 Neighborhood Residential
– Medium Density.

Although the City MR zoning district would most
closely match the zoning to the north, the NR-2
designation is not incompatible with the
surrounding area. Both the MR and NR-2 zones
are applicable to areas designated on the future
land use map of PlanCheyenne as Urban
Residential, which this land is designated as.

Although NR-2 allows slightly more density than
MR, the differences are minor in nature, as both
are intended for a medium amount of density. The applicants are proposing constructing
detached dwellings and MR zone allows detached dwelling lot type classifications of DD5 through
DD7, whereas NR-2 allows detached dwelling lot types of DD6 through DD8. Within Section 5.3
of the UDC, which establishes zoning districts, the MR zone has an intent statement that is very
general in nature:

The MR District is intended for a mix of density and dwelling types within a
neighborhood. All uses require full access to public infrastructure and city services. The
regulations are designed to protect the neighborhood character of the district and protect
the primarily residential use of land.

By comparison, the NR-2 zone has an intent statement that is very similar, but more specific
regarding density:

The NR-2 District is intended for a medium density and mix of dwelling types that
promote a consistent neighborhood character. All uses require full access to public
infrastructure and city services. The regulations are designed to protect the
neighborhood character of the district and protect the primarily residential use of land.
The overall density of this district should be between 10 and 18 dwelling units per acre.

The density the applicants have proposed to staff is within that intended density range at
approximately 10 units per acre, which is congruent with density range of the NR-2 intent
statement. Staff finds that the NR-2 zone is generally appropriate for this property due to
congruence with the comprehensive plan and the intent statement of the NR-2 zone.

