3. ZONING: BASIC STANDARDS & USES - The suitability of the subject property for development according
to the standards and uses of the existing zoning district, compared to the suitability of the subject
property for development according to the standards and uses of the proposed zoning district.
This property is currently within the County MR zoning district. If the Governing Body approves
the annexation of this property, it would need to be rezoned to a City zone before any
development activity could commence.

4. DEVELOPMENT POTENTIAL - The ability of the subject property and surrounding vicinity to be developed
per the Subdivision Standards in Article 4 considering the proposed zoning.
The property could be developed per the Subdivision Standards in Article 4. The residential
portion of the subject property could develop within the standards of the NR-2 zoning district
after the land is replatted into lots that are suitable for development.

5. ZONING: INTENT, APPLICABILITY, & DESIGN OBJECTIVES - The ability of the application to support the
Intent and Applicability statements of the proposed zoning district, and achieve the Design Objectives of
any specific standards.
This application can support the intent and applicability statements of the NR-2 Neighborhood
Residential – Medium Density zoning district, as stated earlier within this report. The NR-2 zoning
district is subject to the Residential Design Standards of Section 6.6, which development on this
area can meet after the property is replatted.

6. ZONING: FUTURE DEVELOPMENT - The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable
standards for development in the proposed zoning district, including other development options in the
event that any specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after
approval.
The subject property would be able to meet all the applicable standards if this application is
approved. Development options would not be limited by this zone change in the event this
specific proposal is not executed. If the specific proposal associated with this application is not
executed, another developer could continue with a similar proposal or replat the property to suit
their needs.

 7. PUBLIC HEALTH, SAFETY, AND WELFARE – Whether the application will harm the public health, safety,
or general welfare.
Wyoming State Section 15-1-103 (a) (xli) grants authority to municipalities to adopt ordinances
which generally promote the public health, safety and general welfare of its citizens. This zone
change would not harm the public health, safety, or welfare of the public.
